  "note": "Wiring resolved by hand (programs.json wiresEffective is null): the 38-line basic sequence recognition circuit as printed on p.53 (this printing reads 6A/6J; set-identical to the VI-SEQREC record) plus the six lines of the p.53 example path 'from 8 to 1 to 2 to 9 to 6 to 5' = 44 wires. Game 3 (the replay after the error power cycle) adds the optional p.54 dummy connections M3t/6R, M4t/6S, M7t/6T as cords 45-47 while power is off (\"For example, in the above program, you could add\", p.54); the rewiring block removes them with the six path cords, because the short path reuses M4t. After game 3, with power off, the script removes the six path cords (38-43) and wires the p.54 short path 'from 8 to 9 to 4' as cords 48-50 (M8t/3com, M9t/2com, M4t/1com), following the C2/I-PROBLEMS rewiring pattern. Before play and after an error, relays 4, 5 and 6 are pushed to the left together (relayGrab target 1000, all three on one tick) and released together, per p.54; the page gives no order. The order is load-bearing: relay 4 holds only through 4H/5G and relay 5 only through 5H/6G, so if they are pushed one at a time in the listed order 4, 5, 6, each released before the next, relays 4 and 5 drop and the first touch registers as an error; pushed 6, then 5, then 4, they hold. The short-path error (9 touched first) is the operator's choice. After the short-path error, play resumes by the p.54 instruction alone ('Whenever an error is made, relays 4, 5 and 6 must be pushed to the left before play is resumed'), with no ER-42 power cycle; the error itself drops relays 4-6 in this model. The play wire ('add a programming wire at M+, leaving one end free', p.53) is script cord 44 from M+#1 with a free end; each move re-ends it as a touch on the square's top terminal, per p.54 '(The wire need not be plugged into the terminal; touching the terminal will make the contact.)', then lifts it back to free. ER-42 is a procedure note (re-set by power off, then on), not a wire change, so errata_applied is empty; the script follows it between games. The error game touches 9 after 8 and 1, a path square out of order; the page does not name an error move. Touch and lift durations, the 30 ms post-power-off advance and the 50 ms relay-push hold are not source timing.",
